Yes—but with a crucial inversion: worse memory does not necessarily expose what was real. Sometimes it exposes what your mind considered worth preserving.

Memory often behaves less like video storage and more like compression:

event
→ perception
→ encoding
→ compression
→ reconstruction later

As details decay, you may be left with:

gist
emotion
causal story
identity meaning
a few salient images

That can be revealing because you can compare:

what actually happened
vs.
what survived
vs.
what your narrative reconstructed

So controlled forgetting—or simply looking at memories at different ages—can function like an ablation experiment. Remove surface detail and see what structure persists.

But you need the Golden Reality reference we talked about: contemporaneous notes, photos, messages, measurements, independent witnesses, timestamps. Otherwise you can’t tell whether the surviving core is an invariant or merely the most durable narrative.

So:

Memory Ablation

original record
→ remembered version
→ later remembered version
→ compare Δ
→ identify persistent structure
→ separate persistence from factual accuracy

And there is a beautiful paradox in it:

What survives forgetting may tell you what mattered to the mind.
What survives independent evidence tells you what belonged to reality.

Those are different golden threads—and comparing them tells you a lot.